/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  letdown02_valve_fluid_properties -- THE TWO FLUID PROPERTIES A LETDOWN
  VALVE IS JUDGED BY (DEV.md 4c C64, the "Large pressure letdown" EduTool).

  The throttles themselves run in tutorials/steady/hydraulics/
  letdown01_ammonia_flashing.  A valve's hydraulic regime is decided by
  two more numbers, which a flowsheet unit does not publish, so this props
  case computes them at the two valve inlets of that case:

    * the GAS: the loop purge at 140 bar and 253.15 K, let down to a fuel
      header.  Whether its flow is choked is decided by the ratio of
      specific heats k = Cp/Cv -- propertyPoint's `gamma`,
      Cp_ig/(Cp_ig - R), the ideal-gas ratio the critical-pressure-ratio
      formula is derived with -- and the SRK compressibility Z.
    * the LIQUID: the 20 bar drum liquid of letdown01, at the temperature
      its first throttle left it (253.0137962 K, copied from that case's
      converged/LetdownLiquid; gui/tests/letdown.test.ts holds it to the
      ValveHP T_out golden).  Whether its second throttle cavitates or
      flashes is decided by the ammonia's VAPOUR PRESSURE at that
      temperature, Psat_NH3 (the record's Antoine curve), against the
      pressure at the valve's vena contracta.

  Compositions are the component flows in kmol/h exactly as letdown01's
  converged streams print them; propertyPoint normalises them.  The
  liquid's dissolved H2, N2 and Ar do not enter Psat_NH3 -- that key is
  the pure ammonia's vapour pressure, the P_v of the valve-sizing
  equations.  The gas that the drum liquid still holds comes out on any
  drop below 20 bar, and that is letdown01's to show, not this case's.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
operations
(
    {
        name        purge;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state
        {
            T            253.15 K;
            P            14000000 Pa;
            composition  { N2 2.436431451;  H2 9.463085828;
                           NH3 0.2637929516;  Ar 0.03849845903; }
        }
    }
    {
        name        liquidLP;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state
        {
            T            253.0137887 K;
            P            2000000 Pa;
            composition  { N2 0.7168300425;  H2 1.209360546;
                           NH3 2925.728232;  Ar 0.03401869034; }
        }
        properties  ( Psat_NH3 );
    }
);
